{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The docs sidebar that got laggier the smarter it got</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team replaced the keyword filter in their docs sidebar with semantic search — type "how do I reset my password" and it finds the right page even without those exact words. They built it server-side: every keystroke-driven query got sent to an API, which turned it into an embedding vector, compared it against the docs' precomputed vectors, and sent back the closest matches.
    </p>
    <p>
      It worked, and it felt smart. It also felt slow. Every search round-tripped to the server, 400-700ms depending on the user's connection — for a sidebar covering maybe 40 doc pages that barely ever changed. The old dumb keyword filter, run entirely in the browser, had felt instant by comparison.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The round-trip latency was the visible symptom, but it was the wrong cost to be paying for this corpus: dozens of pages, shipped with the app, rarely changing — exactly the profile where computing the same semantic search entirely client-side (model and vector index downloaded once, then every query runs locally) would have made searches after the first effectively free.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The mistake wasn't choosing server-side embedding search, it was choosing it by default without checking whether this corpus's characteristics justified paying a network round trip on every keystroke. Server-side buys you a corpus that updates without a client redeploy and zero added client bundle weight — neither of which mattered here, since the docs already shipped inside the app bundle.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Where the vectors live changes what you're actually paying for</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Semantic search needs two things at query time: turning the query text into an embedding vector, and comparing that vector against every document's precomputed vector to find the closest matches. Client-side does both in the browser — download a (usually quantized, tens of megabytes) embedding model plus the docs' precomputed vectors once, then every search after that runs locally, no network call. Server-side sends the raw query to an API that does both steps on a server and returns just the matching results — nothing downloaded upfront, but every single search is a round trip.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The two costs are the same shape at opposite ends: client-side pays a one-time cold-start cost (the model download) before the first search can run at all; server-side pays a per-query cost (a network round trip and an embedding call, every time). Which wins depends on how many searches happen and how big the corpus is — a fixed, small doc set favors client-side (pay once, fast forever); a large or frequently-changing corpus favors server-side (nothing to re-ship when the content changes).
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      There's a third variable beyond corpus size: how often it changes. Client-side needs its vector index rebuilt and re-shipped whenever the underlying docs change — fine for content on a release cadence, painful for content that changes hourly. Server-side's vector store can be re-indexed independently of any client deploy. Offline capability is the other real differentiator: client-side search keeps working with zero network at all, something server-side fundamentally can't offer no matter how fast the API responds.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The support sidebar that split the difference</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      The team behind the laggy sidebar didn't throw out server-side search entirely — they kept it for their public help center, which has thousands of articles a support team updates daily, where re-shipping a client bundle on every content edit isn't realistic. For the in-app docs sidebar specifically — around 40 pages, versioned with the app itself — they switched to a client-side embedding model with a precomputed index built at deploy time.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The cutover was per-surface, not global: the sidebar (small, fixed, ships with the app anyway) moved client-side; the public help center (large, updated independently of app releases) stayed server-side. Both use the same underlying embedding technique — the deciding factor was corpus size and change frequency, not which approach felt more current.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      They measured before committing: the client-side model added roughly 25MB, quantized, cached after the first load, to the sidebar's cold-start cost — acceptable because the sidebar isn't the first thing rendered on page load, it lazy-loads on first open. If it had needed to be interactive immediately on page load, that download would have been a much harder trade to justify, and they'd likely have stayed server-side even with a corpus that otherwise fit the client-side profile.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

