{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>The tradeoff doesn't disappear, it just moves</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      The corpus in this demo is small on purpose — eight documents. A client-side index at that size is nothing to download. Real docs sidebars can run to hundreds of pages, and at some point the download gets big enough that even a one-time cost stops being worth it — the answer flips back toward server-side.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      There's no fixed page-count where the answer flips — it depends on the actual model size chosen (a smaller, more quantized model trades search quality for a smaller download), how often the sidebar gets opened per session (amortizing a download over many searches is fine, over one search it isn't), and whether the download can happen ahead of time — prefetched on hover, loaded in the background after the page settles — rather than blocking the first search.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Don't treat this as one static decision either — a hybrid is legitimate: ship a small client-side index for the fixed core of your content (the material that rarely changes and needs to be fast) and fall back to a server-side call for anything outside that fixed set, like recently added content not yet baked into the shipped index. The two approaches aren't mutually exclusive within the same feature.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

